WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.480
Imagine we have a beautiful article with us.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.420
What if we want to make a copy of it?

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.300
The best way would be to make use of a cast or a mould.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.840
Similar strategies are used by ourselves.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.580
The most important task for a cell

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.520
is to synthesize proteins based on the codes in DNA.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.960
But how are these codes read?

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.880
What's the process that helps decode this language?

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.340
And are proteins synthesized merely by the sequence of

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.660
"A T G and C"?

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:39.120
The answer to all questions lies in the central dogma of life.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.380
But what exactly is the central dogma?

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:53.060
Well, DNA makes copies of itself by the process of replication.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.560
Post replication in every cell the code of the DNA is cracked

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.860
and transcribed into a messenger RNA.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:07.000
This mRNA is then read and the code is translated into proteins.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.700
This interesting concept is nothing but the central dogma.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:16.580
We know that the replication process is semiconservative in nature.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.380
So now our focus will be on the two processes,

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.500
namely transcription and translation.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.620
Let's begin with transcription first.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.720
As we can see here,

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.440
the process in which mRNA is synthesized

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:36.920
based on the parent DNA template, is called transcription.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.320
The part or segment of DNA

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.440
which encodes for a particular protein is called gene.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.320
So how are proteins made from the sequence of base pairs in the gene?

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.780
Let's go in the reverse direction now.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.800
Proteins are a specific sequence of amino acids.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:00.320
So the various amino acids come together in a specific series

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.080
to form a chain that makes up the proteins.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:10.240
Now to get the amino acids in a series we need a set of instructions.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.740
These are present in the form of mRNA.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.260
And where does the mRNA come from?

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.340
It is synthesized from a special template.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:25.180
Can you guess what this template is? Give it a shot.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.960
That's right, the template is a gene.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:33.800
So a segment of the DNA that encodes for protein is copied first.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:37.120
The copy is in the form of mRNA.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:41.960
This process of making mRNA is called transcription.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.560
Now can you tell me why this process is needed?

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.410
Well, it's quite simple.

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:52.580
If there is no transcription then how will mRNA be synthesized.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:56.520
And if that's not done, then how will the proteins be made?

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.740
Hence, transcription is vital.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.380
But it's not as easy as it seems.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:06.780
There are several enzymes and sub steps involved in the process.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.740
We are referring to the steps like opening of the

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.700
DNA double helix at a specific point.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.940
Assembling the set of enzymes

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.980
gathering of nucleotides and so on.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:22.620
After all these steps, an mRNA is formed.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:28.920
The mRNA is like an exact copy of the DNA sequence that coats the protein.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:34.120
The only difference between the two, is that mRNA is single stranded

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.680
and the base thymine is replaced by uracil.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:44.620
Now that the mRNA is ready. It's all set to enter the cytoplasm.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:47.280
That is where the next step begins.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.420
It's the translation process.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:56.520
Let's have a look at this interesting process in the upcoming part.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:03.400
Imagine you're appearing for an exam, but the paper presented to you

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:05.960
is in a language that you don't understand.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:08.700
You are prepared for the exam but

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:11.780
interpretation of the questions is a problem.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:13.840
What can be done?

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:17.920
Some help from a translator would work wonders right?

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.160
So translation is the simplest way to solve this problem.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.160
Clearly a wise move would be to decode the

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:28.920
unrecognisable language and then attempt the exam.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:34.280
Now imagine this cell to be in a similar situation.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.080
Let's assume that this cell encounters a set of

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.780
instructions for performing a task.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:44.160
But the instructions are written in a coded language.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.080
So all it needs to do is to translate this language

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:50.080
and then perform the task.

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:54.200
Any idea which process are we talking about?

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:58.440
That's right. We are talking about the translation process.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:03.100
In continuation with the process of transcription from the previous part,

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:08.100
let's now understand the next step of the central dogma of life.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.300
As the name suggests,

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.220
translation means converting something into a simpler

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:17.020
understandable form.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.040
And what's getting translated here?

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:24.500
That's correct. The code hidden in the mRNA molecule.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:29.840
The process of transcription helps in copying the code written on the DNA.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:33.100
That means it forms the mRNA.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.020
Now the mRNA comes out of the nucleus

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:39.320
through the nuclear pore and enters the cytoplasm.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.260
Here the process of translation begins.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:49.160
To begin with let's find out the directionality of this mRNA molecule.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:54.620
This is the 5 prime end of the mRNA and this is the 3 prime end.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.000
And how do we know this?

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.180
Are we randomly stating this?

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:01.440
No, it's not random.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:05.520
We can recognize these ends with the help of specific markers.

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:10.780
The three prime end has a continuous series of the "A" nucleotides.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:14.380
This region is called the poly A tail.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:19.140
The 5 prime end on the other hand has a cap of methyl group.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:22.580
Thus this end is said to be methylated.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:25.740
But why is the directionality needed?

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:28.680
Well, it's extremely important.

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:31.800
The assembly that carries out transcription

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:35.100
binds to the mRNA near the 5 prime end.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:40.240
So how does the assembly know that this is the 5 prime end?

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:43.600
The easiest way to recognize the 5 prime end

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:46.480
is by the presence of this methyl cap.

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:49.740
This is how the assembly understands to bind near the

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:52.960
5 prime end and not to the poly A tail.

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:57.500
Now what exactly is this assembly that we're referring to?

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.820
Well, that's the ribosome unit.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:05.360
Two units of ribosomes, one small and one large,

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:08.200
form an assembly that helps in the process.

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:11.500
Ribosomes are basically a complex assembly

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:15.680
which acts like a molecular machine for protein synthesis.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:21.400
Remember the small dots attached to the surface of the rough endoplasmic reticulum?

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:25.260
We find them in the diagram of a typical cell.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:28.280
These are nothing but Ribosomes.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:31.840
So the ribosomes are usually made up of two subunits

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.440
a smaller and a larger one.

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:39.500
The smaller subunit binds to the mRNA at the initiation site.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:44.880
Once this is done the larger subunit binds and completes the assembly.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:48.320
Now the other type of RNA called

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:52.020
tRNA steps in to get the remaining work done.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:55.720
At the end a chain of amino acids is formed

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:59.260
and released from the ribosomal unit.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:03.720
So doesn't this chain of amino acids look familiar?

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:06.600
You got that right. It's our protein.

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:10.960
The chain is further modified to achieve a proper configuration

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:13.860
so that the protein becomes functional.

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:18.020
This is how the translation process works.

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:21.100
The code of mRNA is translated

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:24.340
and the protein chain is synthesized out of it.

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:28.220
The process of transcription and translation together

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:30.920
are called as gene expression.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:34.360
The code of a gene is transcribed first

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:37.960
and then translated into the form of proteins.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:41.660
There is an extremely important thing to note here.

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:47.520
Whatever we've talked so far is all about the processes in eukaryotes.

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:53.000
Such high level of organization is found only in eukaryotes.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:56.980
But we know what eukaryotes are right?

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:00.580
A cell with a well defined nucleus

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:04.620
and a highly organised system is a eukaryotic cell.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:09.440
On the other hand cells which lack of proper membrane bound nucleus

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:14.180
and organization are called prokaryotic cells.

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:18.620
Since they lack the proper membrane bound organelles and nucleus

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:23.420
they do not undergo such complex and sophisticated processes.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:29.680
So the replication process in prokaryotes is slightly different.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:32.860
This was a brief idea about the transcription

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:36.820
and translation process as seen in the eukaryotes.

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:41.500
For more interesting concepts in genetics, Tune in to our channel

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:44.980
and don't forget to subscribe.

